1.The debate as to whether nuisance is solely an intentional tort or is better understood as either intentional, or negligent, or the result of an abnormally dangerous activity is of long duration. Part of Dean Keeton’s reasoning in calling nuisance an intentional tort is the fact that in almost all situations the defendant continues its course of conduct even after a complaint by the plaintiff. In this sense, defendant’s continuing behavior is intentional, i.e. the defendant intends to engage in conduct knowing the plaintiff perceives this to be an unreasonable invasion. From this perspective, a negligent nuisance and intentional nuisance overlap. The Gardiner court recognized this in the following note.

Our holding that there is some evidence supporting the jury’s finding of negligent nuisance should not be construed as rejecting as a matter of law that Crosstex could not have been found liable for intentional nuisance. * * * Here, while there is no evidence that Crosstex desired to create the interference with the Gardiner’s use and enjoyment of their land, there is at least some evidence that Crosstex activated the compressor station and continued to operate it with knowledge that the interference was occurring or was substantially certain to occur. When the evidence “may permit the trier to draw different inferences, so that some jurors would conclude that a defendant acted intentionally while others would conclude that he acted only negligently,” both theories could be submitted to the jury so that it may determine what inferences may reasonably be drawn.

Restatement (Second) of Torts § 825 contains the following relevant observation:

Continuing or recurrent invasions. Most of the litigation over private nuisances involves situations in which there are continuing or recurrent invasions resulting from continuing or recurrent conduct; and the same is true of many public nuisances. In these cases the first invasion resulting from the actor’s conduct may be either intentional or unintentional; but when the conduct is continued after the actor
1031
knows that the invasion is resulting from it, further invasions are intentional.

And what then of nuisances resulting from an abnormally dangerous activity? In Turner, cited in Gardiner, the Texas Supreme Court rejected the idea of a general rule of strict liability for abnormally dangerous activities. However, as the Gardiner opinion indicates, Turner left open the possibility of a nuisance claim based on the fact that the defendant was engaged in an abnormally dangerous activity. Insofar as this is the case, Texas seems to have adopted a position not dissimilar from that in Great Britain. In Britain, Rylands is viewed as a cause of action related to nuisance, requiring an escape that interferes with another’s enjoyment and use of property. See Transco Plc. v. Stockport Metro. Borough Council [2004] 2 A.C.1; Cambridge Water Co. v. Eastern Counties Leather Plc, [1994] 2 A.C. 264. The logic behind this position is that ordinarily to be actionable nuisances must be intentional in the sense used in this note. If they are not, they are not actionable. One-time accidental invasions are not nuisances. However, there is an exception for accidental invasions arising from abnormally dangerous activities.

2.Nuisances that result solely from the defendant’s negligence may be relatively easy to resolve because if the defendant exercised due care the nuisance would be abated. Restatement (Second) of Torts § 830 reflects this idea.

§ 830.Gravity vs. Utility—Invasion Avoidable

An intentional invasion of another’s interest is the use and enjoyment of land is unreasonable if the harm is significant and it would be practicable for the actor to avoid the harm in whole or in part without undue hardship.

Upon retrial of the Gardiner case, an important question would be whether the defendant could do anything more to reduce the noise and vibrations without simply moving the compressor station. One neighbor testified that a Crosstex representative had told her that “the building really needed to be fully enclosed, but that would cost . . . a lot of money and Crosstex didn’t have the money and that [the representative] wouldn’t want to live by [the station] either.” 451 S.W.3d 150, 172 (Tex.App.2014).

